<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · 1945 · Sub-Saharan Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Malaika</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Swahili love song from East Africa whose name means &quot;angel&quot;. It is one of the best-known Swahili songs in the world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>disputed (most often credited to Adam Salim; also claimed by Fadhili William, Grant Charo and Lucas Tututu)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>disputed (as the lyricist)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Swahili speakers of East Africa (Tanzania and Kenya)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swahili</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Nairobi, Kenya</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1945 (according to the account crediting Adam Salim); first recorded 1959 or 1960</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (authorship disputed; most often credited to Adam Salim (b. 1916, death not known); Fadhili William credited as composer for royalties)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Malaika&quot; is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> love song, possibly the most famous in Tanzania, Kenya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and one of the most widely known Swahili songs in the world. Malaika means &quot;angel&quot; in Swahili, a word long used for a beautiful girl. The title varies from version to version, as &quot;Malaika Nakupenda Malaika&quot;, &quot;Ewe Malaika&quot; or &quot;My Angel&quot;, and so do the words, whose verses are often rearranged, left out or combined.</p><p>Who wrote it is still disputed. Most people credit Adam Salim, a little-known Tanzanian songwriter born in 1916, who by this account composed it in Nairobi in 1945 for his girlfriend Halima Ramadhani Maruwa; their parents disapproved, and Halima was made to marry a wealthy man. The Kenyan singer Fadhili William always claimed the song, describing in detail how he wrote it, and he is recognised as its composer for royalty purposes. The producer Charles Worrod credited it instead to Grant Charo, William's brother-in-law, a claim Charo is not known to have confirmed, and Lucas Tututu of Mombasa has also claimed it.</p><p>Fadhili William was the first to record it, with his band the Jambo Boys, in 1959 or 1960. Charles Worrod re-recorded it at Equator Sound Studios and marketed it until it became known internationally, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miriam-makeba">Miriam Makeba</a>'s early recording, which added a third verse, made it famous across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and then the world. Through her it reached Harry Belafonte, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a>, Usha Uthup and Angélique Kidjo, and it was also recorded by the Brothers Four, Helmut Lotti and Rocco Granata, among many others.</p><p>In 1967 the Swedish group the Hep Stars, who had heard the song from a hotel chef in Nairobi while filming there, recorded it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>. Their single spent five weeks at No. 1 on the Swedish chart Kvällstoppen, the only song wholly in an African language to top a Swedish chart. The German group Boney M. released it in 1981 as the first single from their album Boonoonoonoos; it reached No. 13 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the top ten in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and a &quot;Lambada Remix&quot; followed in 1989. In 2023 the South African group The Soil recorded a version inspired by Makeba's from the 1980s.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A love song, tender and regretful in mood.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="1404654" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn sg-genius-btn-en" data-genius="4499258" data-what="English translation">Show the English translation from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics and their translation in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Miriam-makeba-malaika-original-single-1974-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a> &middot; <a href="https://genius.com/Genius-english-translations-malaika-miriam-makeba-english-translation-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">its English translation</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="Q1UID0vEeqI" data-title="Malaika: Miriam Makeba, live (1969)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Miriam Makeba, live (1969)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Miriam Makeba Official Channel. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q1UID0vEeqI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Malaika (Miriam Makeba)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording, with a third verse</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Malaika (the Hep Stars)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1967</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording, No. 1 in Sweden</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Malaika (Boney M.)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1981</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording; the remix closes with a chant borrowed from &quot;The Lion Sleeps Tonight&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Malaika (Lambada Remix) (Boney M.)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1989</span><span class="sg-chain-h">re-recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Malaika (The Soil)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2023</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording inspired by Makeba's 1980s version</span></li></ul></section>
